  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Monday...\n\nA shortwave under northwest flow is expected to bring scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms to the region this afternoon and\nevening. Looking at moisture/dewpoints across the region compared\nto models, it appears that there is more moisture than models are\nshowing. This would indicate that instability/CAPE values may be\nslightly underdone. Models are currently showing MUCAPE values of\n800-1200 J/kg and 30-40 kts of bulk shear. This, along with \nprecipitable water values near an inch, the main risks with any \nstrong to severe thunderstorm will be heavy rain, large hail and \nstrong winds. Short term ensembles are in good agreement that \nthunderstorms will initiate of the Little Belt and Snowy mountains\nand the Beartooth/Absaroka mountains early afternoon tracking\neast/southeast, similar to previous days. One limiting factor to \nthunderstorm initiation off the Beartooth/Absarokas will likely \nbe from the line of persistent showers along the foothills that's\nbeen present through the morning. \n\nThe transition into a more westerly flow aloft is expected to\nbring smoke and hazy conditions to the region Wednesday. Subsidence\nand ridging behind the shortwave will transition the region into \na warmer and dryer pattern for the end of the week. Looks towards \nthe weekend, ensembles are hinting at a shortwave moving through \nthe northern portion of the state, but there is still uncertainty \non the timing. If this scenario were to play out, there would be a\nconcern for breezy conditions creating fire weather concerns \ngiven the already warm and dry conditions. This will be something \nto watch through the week. In the meantime, be careful not to \ncause a spark. \n\nHigh temperatures will be in the 80s to low 90s through the\nperiod. TS",
